{% extends layout %}
{% block page_body %}
	{% set ph_kicker = 'Company' %}
	{% set ph_title = 'Company workspace' %}
	{% set ph_lead = 'Every company section in one place.' %}
	{% include '@platform/partials/page-head.html.twig' %}
{#
Generated by: Claude Code CLI
Date time: 2026/08/29 14:32 SAST
Version: 0.5.8.3
Description:
  Consolidated Company workspace.

  Renders every company section the reader may see as one accordion, each section also having a
  semantic standalone route reached through the link in its content area. The section list,
  its order and its permission filtering are resolved by the server from CompanySectionRegistry,
  so this page never names a section and never decides who may see one.

  The accordion markup is the shared core contract used by the Administration, Account and
  Company workspaces alike. The cl-admin-section class family is deliberate: equivalent UI
  patterns reuse one set of core classes rather than growing a per-family variant, and both
  ThemeUiContractTest and CompanyWorkspaceContractTest assert these tokens.

  The selected-company control sits above the accordion so a platform administrator can change
  which company is in context before opening any section.

  WARNING: a template comment is not a safe place to document syntax. F3 parses its own tags
  inside comments, so a tag written out as a usage example becomes a real tag in the compiled
  template. A comment delimiter written inside a comment body ends it early and leaks the
  remainder onto the page. Keep every comment plain prose: no angle brackets, no comment
  delimiters, no template tokens and no code, not even to document one.
Changelog:
  2026/08/29 14:32 SAST
  - Added the required file metadata header and converted indentation to tabs.
  2026/08/25 14:29 SAST
  - Stage D: included the selected-company control above the section accordion.
#}
	<section class="cl-company-workspace" data-company-workspace>
		{% include '@platform/partials/company-context.html.twig' %}
		{% for section in company_sections %}
{#
			The section fetches its own table the first time it is opened. The request attributes
			sit on the details element itself, which is the element that fires the toggle event: an
			earlier version put them on the inner placeholder and listened through a from selector,
			and the request never fired at all, so every section sat on Loading forever.

			The attributes are literal markup with only the route interpolated. Built as a string -
			'hx-get="' ~ route ~ '"' - Twig autoescapes the quotes and the browser receives
			hx-get=&quot;...&quot; as text, so the request never fired for a second reason on top
			of the first. Never assemble an attribute as an expression.
		#}
			<details class="cl-admin-section" data-company-section="{{ section.key }}" {{ section.key == 'dashboard' ? 'open' : '' }}
				{% if section.key != 'dashboard' %}
					hx-get="{{ section.route }}"
					hx-trigger="toggle once"
					hx-target="find .cl-admin-section-lazy"
					hx-select=".cl-section-body"
					hx-swap="innerHTML"
				{% endif %}>
				<summary class="cl-admin-section-summary">
					<span class="cl-admin-section-title">{{ section.label }}</span>
					<span class="cl-admin-section-description">{{ section.description }}</span>
					<span class="cl-admin-section-toggle" aria-hidden="true">⌄</span>
				</summary>
				<div class="cl-admin-section-content">
					<div class="cl-admin-section-actions">
						<a class="cl-admin-section-link" href="{{ section.route }}">Open as standalone page</a>
					</div>
					{% if section.key=='dashboard' %}
						{{ section.content|raw }}
					{% else %}
						<div class="cl-admin-section-lazy">
							<p class="help cl-admin-section-loading">
								Loading {{ section.label }}…
							</p>
							<noscript>
								<p class="help">
									Open <a href="{{ section.route }}">{{ section.label }}</a> to see this section.
								</p>
							</noscript>
						</div>
					{% endif %}
				</div>
			</details>
		{% endfor %}
	</section>
{% endblock %}
